A||||19||Ditz88GT||Help Please..Low oil pressure!||||5||
Z||000000||Ditz88GT||10-19-2001||02:06 PM||||Well, my oil pressure gauge says low anyway.  If fact at idle it even sets the light off, but the engine doesn't sound any different.  I would expect the knocking and stuff to happen, but it still runs and sounds very strong.  Could the gauge be going out?  I replaced the sending unit about 6 months ago and the oil pressure always hung out around 40 or so.  But now I'm guessing it to be around 25-30 on average and like I said the light comes on at idle.  I'm hoping it's not an oil pump, but that is what I'm leaning towards.  Any input would be great.<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>~Ditz88GT<P>E-mail: cditzler@hotmail.com<BR>AIM: Ditz88GT<BR>http://go.to/fiero||||reg||5
Z||000001||California Kid||10-19-2001||03:48 PM||||What's the history on the engine and how many miles are on it???||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ditz88GT||10-19-2001||04:17 PM||||It's a 2.8 with 113K, and like I said it runs very solid.  I don't have a way to manually check the pressure, but is there a way to use the sending unit with a multitester to tell if the gauge is close?  My daily driver is down with a bad trans so I'm driving this thing with low pressure everyday, so I'm more than a little concerned.<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>~Ditz88GT<P>E-mail: cditzler@hotmail.com<BR>AIM: Ditz88GT<BR>http://go.to/fiero||||reg||1
Z||000003||California Kid||10-19-2001||08:33 PM||||Hate to say this, but at those miles the piston rings/crank bearings/oil pump are getting pretty border line, especially if oil changes weren't regular. If it isn't using more than a quart in 3,000 mile, it could be the crank bearings or oil pump just have too much wear. To really be sure about the pressure, I'd locate the sending unit on the engine, take it off, and hook up a mechanical oil pressure gage temporary and see what it reads under all conditions.<P>Someone else on the forum might have some better advice, anyway this post will bump it to the top. <p>[This message has been edited by California Kid (edited 10-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||skitime||10-19-2001||10:18 PM||||Hey Ditz,  Sorry to hear about your problem.  I can check with Jim to see it he has a mechanical guage.  As a last resort, I have two 88 GT engines for sale. Stop over some time for a brewsky and we can ponder upon your problem. <P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Black86SE||10-19-2001||10:25 PM||||I am having the same exact problem with my '86 SE. While sitting still the pressure sets the light off. The only time that the guage says 40psi is when I am accelerating. I have read that it should be around 40 even at idle. My car has 108k miles. But I haven't noticed it burning oil.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image2_4.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>1986 V6 SE<BR>Black<BR>17" Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS||||reg||1
Z||000006||California Kid||10-19-2001||11:10 PM||||Burning oil is normally caused by the valve stem seals (rubber) hardening, or very worn/cracked piston rings. Bad valve stem seals usually show a little smoke out the exhaust on cold start up, after the engine warms up, then you would notice a little puff of smoke between shifts on a manual trans car. Bad piston rings can be found by doing a compression test on each cylinder.<P>This items don't appear to be the case for you guys, as you haven't mentioned either. It can be either the main/rod bearings on the crank wore out, or oil pump, making the assumption that you're getting correct readings from your oil pressure gage. Mechanical oil pressure gages aren't that expensive and it might be a good idea to keep the mechanical hooked up in the engine compartment for reference (as well as keeping the electronic one hooked up once you're sure it working correctly), by using a metal "T" fitting.||||reg||1
