Z||000013||rogergarrison||10-20-2001||09:28 PM||||ive had cars that barely had any oil pressure at idle and they ran 100,000 miles llike that. as long as it has some, and at least 30 driving you should be alright for at least a while. Make sure your gauge is correct (fiero isnt very good) and sending unit. I had a chrysler that showed no more than 15-20 pnds driving, changed sending unit and it went to 50 pnds. Some possiblities are clogged pump screen, bad pump, dirty oil, oil too thin or contaminated with fuel. If all these are ok, its usually worn bearings and your due a rebuild.||||reg||1
Z||000014||camon||10-20-2001||11:59 PM||||Skitime:<BR>I only rescently got another fiero (about a month ago) and only started visiting this board. I use to have an '86 Fiero SE (2.8)red with black trim about 10 years ago. Well that car gave me all kinds of trouble (or maybe it was just my teenage youth being way to hard on it....heck it only had 20,000 miles when I got it so it must have been me).<BR>Anyway I just got your PM and i'll need to stop up sometime (I know exactly where you are).<P>As for my squeal (it was alternator related I think). I remembered I changed my themostat on Monday and remembered antifreeze splashed down the engine and Tuesday is when I noticed the squeal so I thought maybe the antifreeze slashed on the Alternator pulley. So I figured I'd spray it with WD-40 and try to lube the shaft. Well it hasn't made a peep since.<P>I do still have a low oil pressure reading though. It hangs right around the first line after red at an idle when warm. While driving it is above 40psi. When cold I get like 60-80psi on idle and while driving. Maybe I should do an oil change (10w30) and if that doesn't fix it maybe switch to a 10w40 (don't know when oil was changed last). I should also get a manual oil guage to test it. Well just wanted to fill you guys in.<P>Later on!||||reg||1
