Z||000017||Ditz88GT||12-28-2001||02:31 PM||||Well, new oil pump, but still low pressure!  I'm thinking the gauge is bad, maybe....  I have a question, does the light work off of the same sending unit?  Does the gauge set off the light once it hits the red area on the gauge or is the light independent of the gauge?<P><P>------------------<BR>~Ditz88GT<P>E-mail: cditzler@hotmail.com<BR>AIM: Ditz88GT<BR>http://go.to/fiero||||reg||9
Z||000018||StuGood||12-28-2001||04:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ditz88GT:<BR><B>Well, new oil pump, but still low pressure!...does the light work off of the same sending unit?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>If you have an internal oil leak (e.g. - worn bearings, etc.), new oil pump will not cure the low pressure symptom.  In other words, if bearing clearances are too great, they will leak off the oil pressure even with a new pump.  Not speaking from experience here, just from reasoning.<P>To answer your other question: The oil pressure warning light works off the very same sending unit as the gage.  As a matter of fact, the light works off the same <B>signal</B> as the gage (the center terminal on the O.P. sending unit).  There is no separate switch to turn the warning light on.    <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Jake_Dragon||12-28-2001||06:09 PM||||What oil are you using?<BR>I had low pressure in my GT "15w40", the last change I put in straight 40w and now have good pressure.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jake_sig.GIF"> ||||reg||1
